This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

**Version Used**: 

```
Insiders [12224.323]
```

**Steps to Reproduce**:

Compile and run the following code:
```csharp
#nullable enable

var s = new S1("");
_ = s.Value.ToString();

class C1 { public static implicit operator C1?(string s) => null; }

[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Union]
class S1
{
  public S1(C1? x) => Value = x;
  public object? Value { get; set; }
}

namespace System.Runtime.CompilerServices
{
  public class UnionAttribute : System.Attribute;
}
```

**Diagnostic Id**:

`CS8602`

**Expected Behavior**:
`CS8602 Dereference of a possibly null reference` is reported for `s.Value.ToString()` 

**Actual Behavior**:
No warnings at all in the code above.
It crashes at runtime with a `NullReferenceException`

**Notes**
It seems that Roslyn doesn't process the user-defined conversion which might return a nullable value and just uses the non-nullable state of the argument.

An inverse example can be constructed to achieve a false positive warning as well - here the actual value passed to the constructor can't be nullable according to the implicit conversion operator's return type, but the compiler reports a false positive warning (the code runs without problems):
```csharp
#nullable enable

var s = new S1((string?)null);
_ = s.Value.ToString(); // false positive CS8602

class C1 { public static implicit operator C1(string? s) => new(); }

[System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Union]
class S1
{
  public S1(C1 x) => Value = x;
  public object? Value { get; set; }
}

namespace System.Runtime.CompilerServices
{
  public class UnionAttribute : System.Attribute;
}
```

**Version used:** Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp 5.9.0, LanguageVersion.Preview

**Steps to reproduce**

```csharp
var src = @"
namespace System.Runtime.CompilerServices
{
    [AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class | AttributeTargets.Struct)]
    public class UnionAttribute : Attribute { }
    public interface IUnion { object Value { get; } }
}
public union Pet(int, string)
{
    public void M() { }
}
public record R(int X)
{
    public void N() { }
}
";
// Compile with LanguageVersion.Preview and run this analyzer:
c.RegisterSyntaxNodeAction(
    x => x.ReportDiagnostic(Diagnostic.Create(D, x.Node.GetLocation(), x.Node.Kind(), x.ContainingSymbol)),
    SyntaxKind.MethodDeclaration, SyntaxKind.UnionDeclaration, SyntaxKind.RecordDeclaration);
```

**Expected behavior:** one callback per node, as for the record:

```
(12,1): callback for RecordDeclaration (containing symbol R)
(14,5): callback for MethodDeclaration (containing symbol R.N())
```

**Actual behavior:** the union declaration gets two callbacks, and the method inside it three:

```
(8,1): callback for UnionDeclaration (containing symbol Pet)
(8,1): callback for UnionDeclaration (containing symbol <global namespace>)
(10,5): callback for MethodDeclaration (containing symbol Pet.M())
(10,5): callback for MethodDeclaration (containing symbol Pet)
(10,5): callback for MethodDeclaration (containing symbol <global namespace>)
```

This looks like the same issue fixed for positional records in #53136 / #58482: the union's parameter list seems to make the driver analyze the declaration again for the synthesized members. Analyzers that report on union declarations or their members report every diagnostic two or three times. StyleCop Analyzers is holding back union support in several rules because of this (DotNetAnalyzers/StyleCopAnalyzers#4182).

There appears to be an issue with tag helpers that use ParentTag as part of their descriptor. They simply do not execute.
Notably, Intellisense still recognizes them and provides the correct highlighting.
I've worked around it by adding descriptors that do not rely on ParentTag, but it makes the markup less elegant and the diagnosis is hard to figure out without prior knowledge, since it fails completely silently.

I'm pretty sure this is a regression, it used to work before a VS update. My git log says I applied the workaround on August 17th 2026, so the regression probably happened sometime in the week prior to that.

