Found by @EgorBo. Related to https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/85965 and https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/84563.

Discarded assignment of `this` doesn't throw null ref exception in the following scenario:
```
using System;
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;

ref S2 s2 = ref Unsafe.NullRef<S2>();

try
{
    s2.M();
    Console.WriteLine("ERROR: Should have thrown NullReferenceException");
}
catch (NullReferenceException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("PASS");
}

struct S2
{
    public void M() 
    {
        _ = this;
    }
}
```

Behavior is the same for Debug or Release build.

At the same time ```var x = this;``` throws in Debug build, but not in Release.

Unused field value of off `this` doesn't throw. Unused field ref likely has the same behavior.

Presumably a null reference exception should be called when a method is invoked on a null reference, even when the method doesn't make use of `this`. Like in scenario below.
```
using System;
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;

ref S2 s2 = ref Unsafe.NullRef<S2>();

try
{
    s2.ToString();
    Console.WriteLine("ERROR: Should have thrown NullReferenceException");
}
catch (NullReferenceException)
{
    Console.WriteLine("PASS");
}

struct S2
{
    public override string ToString() => "";
}
```

The null ref is thrown because compiler emits `callvirt` instruction. In the examples above `call` instruction is used.

- It feels like the right thing to do would be always using `callvirt` instruction. This way we could continue assuming that an unused `this` access isn't side-effecting. That will leave a hole for scenario when the method is invoked by means other than C# code.
- Or we could do both: use `callvirt` and dereference unused `this` inside a method body.
- Or could leave invocations as is and dereference unused `this` inside a method body. That will leave a "hole" for scenario when method doesn't use `this`.

Somewhat related. It looks like an assignment to a null ref doesn't cause a null ref exception. In the examples above that would be a code like this: ```s2 = default```, ```s2 = new S2()```, ```s2 = new S2(1)```, etc. Not clear whether runtime is responsible for throwing in scenarios like that, or compiler should emit code that would throw. Or the current behavior is expected/desirable. Note, that it looks like an in-place user constructor invocation isn't used for these scenarios, but it is quite possible that in some scenarios it might be used.
